Z||000008||84Bill||07-16-2001||02:56 PM||||My Corsica was "bumped" this weekend. I had just started the car when I felt the car rock a bit, I looked behind me to see an idiot had swiped the rear quarter trieing to pull into the spot next to me! Damage was minimal, scuffed paint and a small rippel in the sheet metal but you know, some people!<BR>The car has been door dinged hundreds of times been rearended  twice and been side swiped by a Neon and the driver just drove off, I got the plate and the cops found the guy but did not cite him for hit and run since he was 70 GRRRRR!!! so I only get 150 of 650 dollars damage due to the deductable. all this in the 3 years I've owned the car.||||reg||8
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||07-16-2001||03:18 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>Ken, why do you think the Firebird owner was a "bonehead" for trying to find out what spineless piece of $hit ran into his car?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>  it wasnt for trying to find out who it was - it was the way he went about it - yelling at everyone as if we had all hit his car - or we all conspired together somehow - the guy had totally lost his cool - demanding that everyone open their garages RIGHT NOW so he could inspect our cars - God help anyone if they woulda happened to be walking around with a can of Comet and a sponge in their hand that morning.<P>  theres a lot to be said about remaining cool and calm in a difficult situation - check out the cars in the lot in as subtle a manner as you can.<P>  And if you dont find out who did it then right it off to the cost of vehicle ownership and let it go - seriously, you will live longer if you dont stew over things like metal, plastic, glass and paint.<P>  I came out of my apt one morning and looked at my car, and WTF?!?! it had dents in one side, and it was all dirty, and the windshield was cracked and Im thinking - did some kids steal my VW Rabbit last night and take it for a joyride through a corn field - and then bring it back?!<P>  then I realized - it wasnt my car :-0<P>  mine was parked at the other end of the parking lot.<P><BR>  EDIT: im not immune to this either - someone scratched up a window on my 2000 saturn SL one week after I bought it in a down town parking ramp - the car was BRAND NEW - still on the first tank of gas - soemone musta did it with a diamond ring<P>   I was pissed - I know that feeling - the trick is to get really pissed - if you feeling like yelling at the top of your lungs then let it rip! then go out running or workout for a couple hours - get it out of your system - and get over it.<P>  after that its just a couple of numbers on a check to the body shop, or on a check from the perp that got caught.<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-16-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||07-16-2001||03:25 PM||||I try to park where people wont be beside me, but thats not always possible. I never ever take my Ferrari to an apt complex unless im going to be standing with it. Years ago I used to have a 77 lincoln, that was a tank. A few times stopped at red lites people ran into me. Id just put it in reverse and back harder into them. You couldnt dent those bumpers. One guy in a Torino really wacked me, i hit him back so hard it blew his radiator. I waved as I drove off. Whats he gonna do report me, Id just say he ran into me.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ken Wittlief||07-16-2001||03:28 PM||||now thats funny! I always thought it would be cool to have a 6 foot long hydralic spike on both ends of your car - so when someone bumps into your car you can puncture his, then retract the spike - taking out their radiator in the front, or the gas tank in the rear.<P>  trailer hitches serve that function well too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierochic88||07-16-2001||04:04 PM||||Yeah I know how you feel but sometimes I don't know who to be madder at!  My mom seems to have a thing for taking my dad's nearly mint 85 GT (which I help to maintain) into the city to work...now she has her own Fiero, which has been banged up numerous times (and repaired as many times) by herself and hit by others...but she still insists on borrowing my dad's.  I had it all cleaned up for Carlisle 2 weeks ago and the car won 1st! :-)  2 days later she takes it to work and it comes home with a huge scratch on the rear corner of the fender!  ARRRGGGHHHH!  She gives me this innocent...well it could have been hit at the grocery store...yeah right not like that!!!   Oh well, I guess it's just a car....a very nice car...but still a car.<P>Jennifer<P>------------------<BR>IM Username:  FieroChic88<BR>Silver 1988 GT<BR>Silver 1986 2m4<BR>Cobalt Blue 85 GT<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/jenscar-sig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||StuGood||07-16-2001||05:30 PM||||My own personal defense against parking lot offenders is to try to be extremely careful with others' vehicles, and to take responsibility when I screw up.  Sound stupid?<P>Maybe it is, but I don't care.  One time, a long time ago, my folks went out for their anniversary and when they came back out to their car, found a long crease (about 1/3 of the car's length, if I recall) along one side.  So whoever it was, had parked in the next spot, and accidentally hit it as they backed out.<P>Dad thought, "Well, there's no way we'll find out who it was - they're long gone." But under the windshield wiper he saw a note, which gave the lady's name, phone number, what happened, and so forth.  After the repairs were done, she (or more likely, her insurance or whatever) paid for the whole thing.<P>About a year ago, I was getting ready to get into my gold Fiero, and noticed what I thought was a little smudge (of mud or something) on the door.  I bent down to look at it, and a lady came up behind me and said, "I was in a hurry to leave, and I must've hit your car."  So she wrote her name and so forth on a piece of paper and apologized and said she'd pay for getting it repainted.  I said something like, "Well - be glad you hit a Fiero.  Otherwise, there'd be a dent where that little paint smear is!"<P>I still haven't gotten it painted yet (might want to repaint the whole car and have her pay for part of it - haven't decided).  But I have no doubts about her sincerety.  For one thing, she could have been long gone - but she actually stopped and was checking to see if she hit my car, even though she was in a hurry.  And I might just tell her to forget the whole thing, because she was honest about it.<P>I think Fret Naught posted a similar story.<P>Not trying to be (too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">) preachy or anything- just remembering some of the good things people have done.  Despite what some people might say, I believe we teach others by example, mostly.   ||||reg||3
Z||000014||ChadMan||07-16-2001||10:07 PM||||Well, now we're getting somewhere...my other neighbor (on the other side -- couldn't have been him) said he saw a white Volvo parked in the spot beside mine on Saturday afternoon while my wife and I were away at a party.  And the scuff on my car is white.  Interesting...and I'm really glad it probably wasn't my neighbor who did it.<P>At my old apartment complex a couple of years ago, I got a knock on my door at 7:30 a.m. on a Sunday.  It was some poor guy with a rusty Dodge Diplomat with his wife and kid...he said he had backed into my car and he was really sorry.  There was a big scuff on my back bumper.  He said I could file a claim with his insurance if I wanted to.  I was upset, but I told him I appreciated his honesty and he could forget about it.  It took me half the day to buff it out, but I didn't want to file an insurance claim against an honest man who's so poor he makes me look like Warren Friggin' Buffett.<P>So see -- I'm not a total ******* .   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But I will be keeping an eye out for white visitor cars with maroon scuffs on the bumper all the same.||||reg||1
Z||000015||AusFiero||07-17-2001||02:21 AM||||Bad luck dude but really it is not worth the stress. Having owned 46 cars and watched most of them cop some sort of parking lot damage I have found it is just a fact of life and something that is hard to prove unless you catch the person in the act.<P>Why they always hit the nice ones though I have never worked out. Drive junkers I have had and never a scratch, go figure.<P>Well ok I am probably paranoid now. The Fiero lives in a climate controlled garage and never ever ever gets parked anywhere but car shows, places where I can see it, or in motel car parks (at shows) full of similar quality cars with owners careful about damage.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||9
Z||000016||Ken Wittlief||07-17-2001||10:29 AM||||The was a parking lot incident at the complex where I lived when I was in college. We all had one car garages in our basements, the doors were behind the building - below ground level.<P>  one night someone parked a volvo right infront of the neighbors garage door, sideways - like it was a parking lot and the garages werent there - it was an Iranian students garage.<P>   about 8AM the student opens his garage door and sees he is parked in -he blows the horn on his car for 2 minutes - which got my attention - but whoever owned the volvo didnt come out.<P>  so the kid gets in his car, starts it up, and rams the volvo - rammed it good - right in the passenger door - I dont know what he thought he was doing - maybe he thought he could push it out of the way?!<P>  then he gets out and realizes he just did about $3,000 damage to the volvo, pulls his car back into his garage and closes the door.<P>  About two hours later the owner of the volvo comes out and he is looking at his car wondering what the h#ll happened to it - I was pulling out of my garage on the other side.<P>  he asks me "how could this have happened?!" I looked at it and said " you didnt park infront of someones garage? DID YOU?!" the tone of my voice implied he had commited an offense that was worse than what he had received (his car was about 1 foot from the garage door - it was obvious he had parked right in front of someones garage).<P>   Without him saying so I quickly realized the guy was having an affair with someone in the apartment complex - and he wasnt suppose to have been spending the night there - he must of told his wife he was out of town - so he couldnt call the police<P>  or complain<P>   or do anything!!!<P>  so in a strange twist of fate the flared up anger of an Iranian graduate student brought about an ironic form of parking lot justice - never saw or heard from the guy with the volvo again.||||reg||1
Z||000017||84Bill||07-17-2001||11:04 AM||||Hehhe cool Ken<BR>I used to live behind a restraunt and people would always park in our driveway. I had a great time thinking up devious ideas on what to do to their cars. One time theese 2 guys parked in my driveway looked right at me while I was doing yard work and just walked away. I was rather miffed and took the garden hose and shoved it in the window and let it run for about 20 minutes.<BR>Another time I blocked a car (my preferd method)in my driveway by pulling in behind it after an hour I hear the doorbell ring, the person asked me to move my car and I asked "didn't you know that was a private driveway?" the said 'no I thought it was part of the parking lot' I said Oh ok I see in that case thats not my car sorry can't move it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> they almost always called the cops and the police told them they were SOL.<BR>I've dragged cars out of my driveway, flattened tires, keyed paint. I have let people park in my driveway<B>every time  they asked </B> but if the just pull in and walked away their cars were MINE and the cops told me I could do anything I want except burn it. Hell I bought a 77 TBird with 90mph (multi colored) bumpers just for the purpose of bashing cars in my driveway.<BR>||||reg||1
